From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: nomorecoolnicknames <nomorecoolnicknames@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 17:54:16 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] camera: provider@2.4 service: ignore SIGPIPE

A dumpsys reader that closes its end early (dumpsys media.camera | grep -m1)
made the HAL's dump write() raise SIGPIPE; init then restarted the whole
provider and every open camera was lost (Meizu MX6: 'Service
vendor.camera-provider-2-4 (pid 483) received signal 13'). With SIGPIPE
ignored the write fails with EPIPE and the provider keeps running.

Test: with a camera open, 'dumpsys media.camera | head -1' leaves the
      provider pid unchanged and no 'received signal 13' in dmesg.

Change-Id: I3e96e4f77eedf20711e73a9a0a52f6b19a13def6

diff --git a/camera/provider/2.4/default/service.cpp b/camera/provider/2.4/default/service.cpp
index 0a4f787..52201f5 100644
--- a/camera/provider/2.4/default/service.cpp
+++ b/camera/provider/2.4/default/service.cpp
@@ -25,6 +25,7 @@
 #include <cutils/properties.h>
 #include <hidl/LegacySupport.h>
 #include <malloc.h>
+#include <signal.h>
 
 using android::status_t;
 using android::hardware::defaultLazyPassthroughServiceImplementation;
@@ -40,6 +41,11 @@ const bool kLazyService = false;
 int main()
 {
     ALOGI("CameraProvider@2.4 legacy service is starting.");
+    // A dumpsys reader that closes its end early (`dumpsys media.camera | grep -m1`)
+    // made the HAL's dump write() raise SIGPIPE and init restarted the whole
+    // provider, dropping every open camera (Meizu MX6, 2026-10-02 and 2026-10-06,
+    // "received signal 13"). With SIGPIPE ignored the write just fails with EPIPE.
+    signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_IGN);
     // The camera HAL may communicate to other vendor components via
     // /dev/vndbinder
     android::ProcessState::initWithDriver("/dev/vndbinder");
